# Freeze the character tools/keys/prestige-earthsinger.keys builds, so every check that
# runs that script can load him instead of rolling him again.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/prestige-earthsinger-save.keys 3
#
# THE CHARGEN BELOW IS A COPY of tools/keys/prestige-earthsinger.keys lines 1-121,
# deliberately unchanged, and the original is NOT edited. That script builds its
# character inline rather than including a shared chargen-*.keys, so there is
# nothing to @include. Copying rather than refactoring means no existing check
# changes what it measures. The same choice, and the same warning, are in
# tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys: do not "improve" this copy -- the
# fixture is only worth having if it holds THE character that script rolls.
#
# WHY A CHARACTER IS FROZEN AT ALL: see the header of
# tools/keys/chargen-default-save.keys. bd inc-vrt8.

# Build an Earthsinger and photograph two things bd inc-tek.8.3 changed about
# the class: the race gate that refused the gnomes its own refusal message
# names (PA-03-F14), and the Grounded Stance that any pair of boots switched
# off (PA-03-F5).
#
# PA-03-F14. The gate tested dwarves only. Its gnome clauses were commented
# out, and the reason was a naming bug rather than a mechanism failure -- the
# disabled lines named $"Gnome, Rock" and $"Gnome, Deep", against the real
# resource names "Gnome;race" and "Deep Gnome;race". So the class's own live
# refusal message promised "a dwarf, rock gnome or deep gnome" and then
# refused two of the three. This character is a rock gnome, the exact case
# the message named and the gate denied.
#
# PA-03-F5. Grounded Stance adds the character's Earthsinger level to the
# damage of every melee or unarmed hit, provided she is not levitating, not
# ethereal, on earth, and NOT WEARING MAGICAL BOOTS. The test read
#     if (EActor->InSlot(SL_BOOTS) != NULL_OBJ)
# which asks whether the slot is occupied, not whether the boot is magical,
# so ordinary leather boots cancelled the class's signature ability -- and
# nearly every character wears boots, so it was off nearly always. An
# ethereality test was missing too and has been added.
#
# THE ORACLE for PA-03-F5 is the combat message. The effect appends its own
# term to the damage string, ' %+d GS' (lib/prestige.irh:1684), and
# src/Fight.cpp:8532 prints that string in the "Damage:" line. So a hit
# either says GS or it does not, and the character is wearing ordinary boots
# while it happens.
#
# THE BUILD, and why each number is forced.
#   * Race: a gnome, which is the half of the roster that was refused.
#   * Bardic Music, so the class must be entered from the bard.
#   * Melee BAB +5. A bard attacks at 75%, so that is 7th level.
#   * Craft 15. ISkillLevel counts ranks, training and (attribute-11)/2, and
#     the bard's Craft reads Wisdom. Ten ranks is a 7th-level cap, so the
#     bought Wisdom has to carry the rest -- hence 17 bought against the
#     gnome's -2.
#   * Endurance and Hardiness, which are the two feats character generation
#     hands out. Hardiness is only offered once Endurance is held, so the
#     order matters.
#   * No god, or Ekliazeh. Gnomes are given no god at birth, so nothing to do.
#   * Seven core levels plus four prestige levels is 11, MAX_CHAR_LEVEL.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes

@choose "Gnome"
@choose "Gnome (Standard)"
@choose "Bard"

# Point buy, 75 points, spent to the last one.
p
RIGHT*4         # STR 12
DOWN
RIGHT*10        # DEX 18
DOWN
RIGHT*8         # CON 16
DOWN
RIGHT*8         # INT 16
DOWN
RIGHT*9         # WIS 17, which the gnome's -2 leaves at 15, for Craft
DOWN
RIGHT*10        # CHA 18
DOWN
RIGHT*6         # LUC 14
ENTER y         # "Are these your final stats?"

@choose "Neutral"
@choose "Endurance"     # Hardiness needs it, and the class needs both
@choose "Hardiness"
@while "Choose a feat" a

@expect "Unspent Skill Ranks"
@cursorto:mark "Craft" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Alchemy" UP
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Appraise" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Bluff" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Concentration" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Decipher Script" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Diplomacy" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Disguise" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Escape Artist" DOWN
RIGHT*3
@cursorto:mark "Heal" DOWN
RIGHT*3
ENTER y # Done, and confirm keeping the ranks

a       # Personality archetype
@while "Learn which spell" a    # a bard picks spells; any will do here

ESC     # dismiss the arrival-room description; now in play
w       # wizard mode
y       # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
w       # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
DOWN DOWN
SPACE   # Free Advancement: NO -> YES
DOWN*9
SPACE   # Show All Combat Rolls: NO -> YES. This is what makes the engine
        #   print the "Damage:" line (src/Fight.cpp:8532), which is where
        #   Grounded Stance writes its own term.
ESC

d                               # character sheet
# Levelling a bard opens a spell-learning menu at most levels, so the plain
# "press G until the level arrives" shape is not enough on its own.
@until "Class  Bard 7" G
@while "Learn which spell" a

S
@expect "Unspent Skill Ranks"
@cursorto:mark "Craft" DOWN
RIGHT*10
ENTER y

@dump:arrival

# HOW MANY KEYS THE OPENING WANTS IS A PROPERTY OF THE CHARACTER, so no fixed
# count is right for every chargen. Measured 2026-09-17: tools/keys/chargen.keys
# leaves NOTHING pending and reaches the System Menu on the first ESC, while
# tools/keys/chargen-rogue.keys leaves a god's speech and an AutoHide line, so
# the first ESC only dismisses a message. A hard-coded ESC ENTER SPACE, copied
# from tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys, failed in both directions: it
# walked INTO the menu for the barbarian and fell short of it for the rogue.
# @until sends ESC as many times as this character needs and stops the moment
# the menu is up (src/Wposix.cpp:1318-1326).
# CHARGEN IS NOT OVER UNTIL THE ARCHETYPE IS PICKED, and ESC will not dismiss
# that screen -- it is a menu wanting a letter. Measured 2026-09-17: five of
# these save scripts reached "Your personality archetype has little direct
# influence..." and spent all 200 of @until's passes pressing ESC at it
# (src/Wposix.cpp:1568-1571). [a] is The Ambitious Conqueror; the choice only
# has to be the SAME one every time, because a fixture is a frozen character
# and the archetype is, in the game's own words, "largely a 'colour' choice".
@while "personality archetype" a

@until "System Menu" ESC

# @until only warns when it gives up (src/Wposix.cpp:1568-1571), so the
# assertion stays: it stops the run at exit 6 rather than pressing a letter
# into whatever screen is really there. [a] beside [b] is Save and Quit.
@expect "System Menu"
b                       # [b] Save and Continue (src/Player.cpp:843)

# The save is not written by the keypress. Case 2 of the System Menu sets the
# autosave flag and Timeout = 1 (src/Player.cpp:851-853); Game::Play writes the
# file at the top of the next pass of its loop (src/Main.cpp:266-275). This
# dump therefore fires AFTER the file is on disk, and photographs the engine's
# own "Autosave... done." line saying so.
@dump:saved

# NO @quit HERE, DELIBERATELY. @quit becomes KY_CMD_QUICK_QUIT
# (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664), which is Save and Quit: it would write the save a
# SECOND time and shuffle the first copy off to <name>.sav.backup, leaving the
# fixture a file no line of this script asked for. Letting the key script run
# out instead ends the session with exactly one save.
